<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Gracchus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 154–121 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Gaius Sempronius Gracchus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Gracchus was a Roman tribune of the plebs in 123 and 122 BCE who continued the reforms of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. His laws provided subsidised grain for citizens, gave the equestrians control of the extortion court and founded colonies, including Junonia on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. When his opponents moved against him in 121 BCE the Senate passed an emergency decree, and he died with thousands of his followers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus.jpg" alt="Gaius Gracchus" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Baptiste Claude Eugène Guillaume · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi_(cropped)_Gaius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaius Sempronius Gracchus came from a well-connected family. His father, Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus, was consul in 177 and 163 BCE and censor in 169 BCE, and his mother Cornelia, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>, refused a marriage proposal from Ptolemy VIII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to devote herself to her sons.</p><p>He served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> in the Numantine War in 133 BCE and then sat on the land commission distributing public land to poor families that his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> had created. In 126 BCE he supported Marcus Fulvius Flaccus's programme, was elected quaestor and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. He returned early without his commander's permission, answered the censors successfully, rebutted a charge of aiding the revolt at Fregellae and won election as tribune.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 123 BCE Gaius proposed to bar a magistrate deposed by the people from office again, a bill withdrawn at his mother's request, and a law on appeal to the people in capital cases that sent the former consul Publius Popillius Laenas into exile.</p><p>He set a maximum price for grain of six and a third asses for a modius, re-enacted his brother's land law and may have authorised colonies at Scolacium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>. He also moved a law on soldiers' clothing and a law assigning consular provinces before the consular elections. His grain law, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithe</a> in Asia that benefited the publicani, and his rousing oratory made him widely popular, and he was re-elected for 122 BCE.</p><p>In his second tribunate he transferred the jury pool of the extortion court to the equestrians, and his ally Gaius Rubrius carried a law for a colony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. The senate set the tribune Marcus Livius Drusus against him, and a bill to give citizenship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> and Latin rights to Italian allies failed after opposition from the consul Gaius Fannius. Gaius left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to supervise the colony and was defeated when he stood for a third tribunate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 121 BCE a tribune moved to repeal the law for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. After an attendant of the consul Lucius Opimius was stabbed to death, the senate passed the first senatus consultum ultimum. Gracchus and Flaccus armed their followers and seized the temple of Diana on the Aventine, and Opimius marched against them with the militia and Cretan archers. Flaccus and his sons were killed, and Gaius either was killed or committed suicide after crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>.</p><p>A special court tried his supporters, and many were reportedly executed without formal trial. Opimius built a temple of Concord and was acquitted when prosecuted, which legitimised the use of such decrees. Most of Gaius's legislation survived, though the law on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> was repealed, and the question of citizenship for Italians was not raised again for some thirty years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaius Gracchus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The site of Carthage was left empty, and an attempt by <b>Gaius Gracchus</b> to plant a colony named Junonia there in 122 BCE soon failed, although Roman settlers and veterans of Gaius Marius took land elsewhere in the province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…killed in 133 BCE, and in 121 BCE the Senate passed its first “final decree” against his brother <b>Gaius Gracchus</b>; Sulla marched on Rome and made himself dictator, Julius Caesar crossed into Italy with his army in 49 BCE, and after his murder the lex Titia of 43…</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-133" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gaius Gracchus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-133">What was here in 133 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
